WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.848 --> 00:00:08.731 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to North America episode three of three. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.382 --> 00:00:22.049 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Six island countries finish our North America route, across the Bahamas, Cuba,
the two halves of Hispaniola, Jamaica and the islands off Venezuela.

3
00:00:22.716 --> 00:00:25.607 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring The Bahamas and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:28.087 --> 00:00:36.017 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Bahamas are an archipelago in the Atlantic, southeast of Florida and north
of Cuba, spread over hundreds of islands and cays.

5
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:54.523 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name probably comes from the Spanish baja mar, "shallow sea". Columbus's
first landfall in 1492 was on one of its islands, probably the one now called
San Salvador. On the islet of Big Major Cay, pigs swim out to meet the boats.

6
00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:02.680 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture the Bahamas as a scatter of shallow banks north of Cuba, with swimming
pigs on one of the smallest cays.

7
00:01:03.351 --> 00:01:07.356 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Cuba and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:18.322 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean, a long country west of Hispaniola
with Florida to its north and Jamaica to its south.

9
00:01:18.982 --> 00:01:36.298 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Columbus landed here in 1492 and believed it was part of Asia, a belief the
island kept for four hundred years. The American cars of the 1950s still on
its streets date from before the trade embargo of the early 1960s.

10
00:01:36.958 --> 00:01:46.002 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Cuba as the long island where Columbus thought he had reached Asia,
and where 1950s American cars still drive.

11
00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:50.307 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Haiti and its neighbours into view.

12
00:01:52.515 --> 00:02:03.603 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Haiti occupies the western third of the island of Hispaniola, with the
Dominican Republic to its east, Cuba to the west, and the Atlantic and the
Caribbean around it.

13
00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:20.098 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
In 1804, after a revolt of enslaved people, it became the first independent
state founded by former slaves. The Citadelle Laferrière, on a mountaintop in
its north, is the largest fortress in the Americas.

14
00:02:20.758 --> 00:02:31.207 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Haiti as the first state founded by former slaves in 1804, with the
largest fortress in the Americas on its northern mountain.

15
00:02:31.882 --> 00:02:36.956 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Dominican Republic and its neighbours into view.

16
00:02:39.143 --> 00:02:49.894 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Dominican Republic fills the eastern two thirds of Hispaniola, facing
Haiti across the border, with the Atlantic to the north and the Caribbean to
the south.

17
00:02:50.549 --> 00:03:04.149 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for its capital, Santo Domingo, and so for Saint Dominic. Santo
Domingo, founded in 1496, is the oldest city built by Europeans still standing
in the Americas.

18
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:14.355 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hold the Dominican Republic as the eastern side of Hispaniola, home of the
oldest European city still standing in the Americas.

19
00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:18.569 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Jamaica and its neighbours into view.

20
00:03:20.836 --> 00:03:28.127 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Jamaica is an island in the Caribbean Sea, south of Cuba and west of
Hispaniola.

21
00:03:28.789 --> 00:03:45.061 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From the Taíno Xaymaca, often read as "land of wood and water", came its name.
Reggae was born here in the 1960s, and Bob Marley took it around the world.
Blue Mountain coffee is grown on the mountains of its east.

22
00:03:45.718 --> 00:03:55.819 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Jamaica as the small island that gave the world reggae and Blue
Mountain coffee, its mountains rising on the eastern side.

23
00:03:56.566 --> 00:04:01.164 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Trinidad and Tobago and its neighbours into view.

24
00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:18.539 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Trinidad and Tobago lies at the south-eastern edge of the Caribbean, off the
north coast of Venezuela, with Trinidad the larger island and Tobago the
smaller one to its north.

25
00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:39.543 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Trinidad, "Trinity", was named by Columbus in 1498, it is said for three hills
he saw from the sea. The steelpan, made from oil drums, was invented here in
the 20th century, and its Pitch Lake is the largest natural deposit of asphalt
in the world.

26
00:04:40.204 --> 00:04:49.120 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Trinidad and Tobago as the steelpan islands at the Caribbean's
southern corner, with the asphalt lake on Trinidad.

27
00:04:53.295 --> 00:05:06.681 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Bahamas lie north of Cuba. Cuba is the long big island; Haiti and the
Dominican Republic share Hispaniola to its east; Jamaica sits south of Cuba;
Trinidad and Tobago lie off the coast of Venezuela.

28
00:05:07.343 --> 00:05:22.308 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember shallow banks and swimming pigs, an island Columbus thought was Asia,
the first state founded by former slaves, the oldest European city still
standing in the Americas, reggae, and steelpan drums.
